const intro={"n":9,"title":"Fierro Defends the Captive Woman in a Deadly Fight","nums":[584,605,623],"summary":"Fierro sees the captive woman wounded and bound, and faces an Indigenous man armed with boleadoras. During the prolonged confrontation Fierro falls and is almost killed, but the woman pulls his attacker away. Fierro resumes fighting and eventually kills the man. The canto ends with the woman gathering the remains of her murdered child while Fierro helps her. This is the narrator's account of a particular encounter, not evidence about entire Indigenous communities.","people":"Martín Fierro; the captive woman who saves Fierro during the fight; the Indigenous man he kills; the woman's deceased child.","themes":"The captive woman's agency and courage; violence and moral self-presentation; risk and chance; maternal grief; narratorial prejudice.","words":"boleadoras (weighted throwing cords); chiripá (wrapped rural garment); facón (large knife); pingo (horse); cautiva (female captive).","questions":["What does Fierro see when he first approaches the captive woman?","How does the woman intervene when Fierro falls?","What causes the attacker's final loss of footing?","What does the last stanza show about the woman's grief?","How does Fierro's narration shape the reader's judgment of his violence?"],"analysis":"The woman is not simply a passive recipient of rescue: she physically saves Fierro, who then kills their assailant. The child's remains and the mother's grief close the canto. The action should be described without treating Fierro's racist descriptions of his opponent as reliable claims about Indigenous people generally.","related":"/en/magazine/indigenous-peoples-frontier/","relatedTitle":"Indigenous peoples and historical frontier accounts"};
